From: mscharged-port
Subject: Release a destroyed target's EFB copies in native GX
Upstream-base: 7a6bf165d9f1c5914181c079c7d4bfa7204f3509

On the console a target's EFB copies are written into the target's memory, so
destroying a target needs no GX call. Native GX keeps copies in host textures
keyed by the destination address, which stayed allocated after the target was
gone: every match's crowd and captain impostor targets added textures for good.
A later texture at the same address could also be drawn from a stale copy.
DestroyTexture now tells native GX to release them (GXDestroyCopyTex).

diff --git a/src/NL/glx/glxTarget.cpp b/src/NL/glx/glxTarget.cpp
index d8c77fe..0487a0b 100644
--- a/src/NL/glx/glxTarget.cpp
+++ b/src/NL/glx/glxTarget.cpp
@@ -117,6 +117,13 @@ void GLXTarget::ReservedTargetHookB()
 
 void GLXTarget::DestroyTexture(unsigned long hash)
 {
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+    // The console's EFB copies land in this target's memory. Native GX keeps
+    // them in host textures keyed by that address: release them with the
+    // target, so they are freed and cannot stand in for a later texture there.
+    if (mTexture != 0 && mTexture->m_SwizzledData != 0)
+        GXDestroyCopyTex(mTexture->m_SwizzledData);
+#endif
 }
 
 void GLXTarget::ClearDefault()
